```csharp
public synchronized void Execute() 
{
    if (executed) 
    {
        throw new InvalidOperationException("Benchmark was already executed");
    }
    executed = true;
    runData.StartTimeMillis = DateTime.Now.Ticks / TimeSpan.TicksPerMillisecond;
    algorithm.Execute();
}
```

However, C# doesn't directly support the `synchronized` keyword like Java does. You can achieve similar functionality using the `lock` statement. Here's a more idiomatic C# translation:

```csharp
private readonly object _lock = new object();

public void Execute() 
{
    lock (_lock)
    {
        if (executed) 
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Benchmark was already executed");
        }
        executed = true;
        runData.StartTimeMillis = DateTime.Now.Ticks / TimeSpan.TicksPerMillisecond;
        algorithm.Execute();
    }
}
```

Note that I also replaced `IllegalStateException` with `InvalidOperationException`, which is the C# equivalent. I've also assumed that `runData.setStartTimeMillis()` was setting a property or field, so I've used `runData.StartTimeMillis` to set it directly. If it's a method, you'll need to adjust accordingly. 

Additionally, I've used PascalCase for the method name (`Execute`) as per C# naming conventions. I've also assumed that `algorithm.execute()` is a method that can be called directly, so I've used `algorithm.Execute()` to follow C# naming conventions. 

You may also want to consider using `Stopwatch` class to measure time instead of `DateTime.Now.Ticks`, as it is more accurate for measuring elapsed time. 

Here is an example using `Stopwatch`:

```csharp
private readonly object _lock = new object();
private readonly Stopwatch _stopwatch = new Stopwatch();

public void Execute() 
{
    lock (_lock)
    {
        if (executed) 
        {
            throw new InvalidOperationException("Benchmark was already executed");
        }
        executed = true;
        _stopwatch.Start();
        algorithm.Execute();
        _stopwatch.Stop();
        runData.StartTimeMillis = _stopwatch.ElapsedMilliseconds;
    }
}
```

